{# Telephone, matching the client's Tally form.

   ONE input, labelled "Mobile Number (with country code)", pre-filled with the
   default dial code. Tally does exactly this: a single type="tel" field where
   the respondent types the whole international number. Client decision,
   2026-08-23, after comparing it against a two-control version.

   The earlier build split this into a country <select> plus a number input.
   That produced cleaner E.164 but it is not what the client's own form does,
   and a second control is a second thing to tab through. Pre-filling "+254 "
   keeps the benefit that made the split worth trying: nobody has to guess
   whether to write +254, 254, or 0.

   Normalisation still happens in form-handler.js, which strips spaces and
   guarantees a leading +, so the backend keeps receiving one E.164 string.

   WHY NOT libphonenumber: 145KB to validate a field the Secretariat reads by
   eye, and a validator that rejects a real number costs more than a loosely
   formatted one.

   Caller sets: fieldId, fieldRequired. Reads `countries`. #}
<p class="form__row">
  <label class="label" for="{{ fieldId }}">Mobile number (with country code){% if fieldRequired %} <span class="req" aria-hidden="true">*</span>{% endif %}</label>
  <input class="input" id="{{ fieldId }}" name="phone" type="tel"
         inputmode="tel" autocomplete="tel" enterkeyhint="next"
         maxlength="24" value="{{ countries.defaultDial }} "
         placeholder="{{ countries.defaultDial }} 712 345 678"
         aria-describedby="{{ fieldId }}-hint"{% if fieldRequired %} required{% endif %}>
  <span class="form__hint" id="{{ fieldId }}-hint">Include the country code. Replace {{ countries.defaultDial }} if you are outside Kenya.</span>
</p>
